# GatewayGuard -- byte-exact storage, no line-ending translation.
#
# "* -text" tells git to treat every file as binary for line-ending purposes:
# what goes into the repo is exactly what is on disk, byte for byte.
#
# This is deliberate and load-bearing. DefectPreventionPlaybook Class 7 rule 5
# specifies recovery as "restore byte-for-byte, confirm by hash." Git's Windows
# default (core.autocrlf=true) rewrites CRLF to LF on commit and back on
# checkout -- a restored file would then differ from the original and the hash
# check would fail. CodingStandards also require .ps1 builds to be CRLF with a
# UTF-8 BOM; translation would put both at risk.
#
# Belt and braces: core.autocrlf is also set to false in .git/config, but that
# is a local setting that can be changed or lost. This file travels with the
# repository.
* -text
